{"text": "Like all historians. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Social historians draw from a variety of primary sources. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Social history often emphasizes ordinary people. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Rather than elites. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Yet. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> In most places and times. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Non elites lacked access to education. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> And, thus, did not produce substantial amounts of documentation. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Even when they did produce documentation. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> These materials rarely ended up in archives. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Where they could be preserved. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> And made available to researchers. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Because of such biases in the historical record. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Social historians have to be very creative. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> In their work. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Like other historians. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Social historians analyze. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Textual sources produced by government officials, employers, and other elites. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Yet rather than simply take such sources at face value. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> They read them against the grain. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> So, for example. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> A social historian will read legal documents. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Not only for the facts provided by slaveowners. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> She will also examine the silences, interruptions, rumors. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> And other details. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> That offer hints about the enslaved peoples attitudes and actions.", "alignment": {"characters": ["L", "i", "k", "e", " ", "a", "l", "l", " ", "h", "i", "s", "t", "o", "r", "i", "a", "n", "s", ".", " ", "<", "b", "r", "e", "a", "k", " ", "t", "i", "m", "e", "=", "\"", "0", ".", "8", "s", "\"", " ", "/", ">", " ", "S", "o", "c", "i", "a", "l", " ", "h", "i", "s", "t", "o", "r", "i", "a", "n", "s", " ", "d", "r", "a", "w", " ", "f", "r"